  "slug": "cryo-em-66f0",
  "summary": "Sparse labeled data limits generative and structure models for molecules and peptides, prompting tests of randomization and generated-data augmentation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is extending models beyond scarce labeled data. The SAFE study isolates representation choice in low-data molecular generation, reporting that randomizing atom and fragment order raises novel molecule yield 450-fold over canonical SAFE and 2.8-fold over a randomized reference. The protein language model preprint asks how models learn from small protein sets and tests augmentation strategies, including ESM-3 generated complexes. Neopep addresses non-canonical peptide structure with a sequence-conditioned diffusion model pretrained on PDB and fine-tuned with reinforcement learning using machine-learned force field energy. The quantum Wasserstein GAN with a VAE latent space is benchmarked against classical latent GANs for de novo molecule design. What is new is the explicit control of training data form, augmentation, or reward as the lever for performance in these data-constrained settings.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-28"
}
